From 66c7ddecc4638e2d15a0741703b86d778f4b2dda Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ZouYingjun <zouyingjun@live.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 23:37:53 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat: custom URL scheme handler served from a local directory
 (Cocoa)

Add webview_create_from_config() + webview_config_t: a size-prefixed
creation struct equivalent to webview_create() for the debug/window
fields, plus optional scheme/scheme_root. When set, requests to
scheme://<host>/<path> are served from <scheme_root>/<path> by a
WKURLSchemeHandler, so applications can bundle assets behind a virtual
scheme instead of running a local HTTP server.

Registration must happen at creation time: WKWebViewConfiguration
ignores setURLSchemeHandler once the WKWebView exists, which is why
this is a creation parameter rather than a setter. The C++ engines gain
matching defaulted constructor arguments (ignored, for source
compatibility, on GTK/Win32 where the feature is not implemented yet).

Implementation notes:
- One ObjC class (WebviewWKURLSchemeHandler, registered once via
  lookUpClass-then-allocate) is shared by all engine instances; the
  per-instance root directory travels as an associated object.
- Request paths are percent-decoded and confined to the root directory:
  any ".." path component (including the exact "..") is rejected with
  404.
- Common MIME types are detected from the extension so WKWebView
  parses HTML/CSS/JS correctly; everything else defaults to
  application/octet-stream.
- Missing files produce a 404 response; content is delivered in a
  single shot (no incremental streaming), so stopURLSchemeTask: is a
  no-op.
- Handler entry points are noexcept and catch everything: no exception
  may cross into Objective-C.

Verified end to end on macOS: an HTML page + CSS served through the
scheme, fetch status codes observed from inside the page (200 for a
real file, 404 for a traversal attempt, 404 for a missing file).
